+
+### Shared spinor algebra
+
+`topology_symmetry` also provides a Cartesian O(3)-to-SU(2) lift. Improper operations act on spin
+through their axial rotation, `det(R)*R`; orbital inversion parity belongs to the separate AO
+action. The representative spin matrices retain a double-group factor system: they must not be
+treated as an ordinary single-valued representation of the spatial group.
+
+The frame transformation composes an already supplied atom/AO/Bloch action with the spin matrix and,
+for antiunitary maps, physical time reversal $i\sigma_y K$. A product validator checks unitarity and
+$B_g B_h^{(*)}=\omega(g,h)B_{gh}$, with conjugation when $g$ is antiunitary. Supplied factors can
+include both nonsymmorphic Bloch phases and double-group signs.
+
+Tests cover all 48 cubic polar operations through independent Pauli-vector covariance, their
+double-group multiplication factors, a non-real nonsymmorphic phase, and $\Theta^2=-1$. Together
+with metric-aware sewing and endpoint-link transformations these are reusable numerical kernels.
+These kernels alone do not generate space-group AO actions. The independent property reconstruction
+path above combines them with the existing k-point operations. Wilson links remain explicit. The
+Kubo path additionally validates current covariance; see
+[spectral_localizer.md](spectral_localizer.md#property-eigenframe-symmetry).
+
+### Little-group products and projective characters
+
+`topology_little_group` selects operations that preserve a supplied fractional reciprocal vector
+modulo a reciprocal lattice vector. Direct-space operations use the column-lattice convention
+$\mathbf r\mapsto W\mathbf r+\tau$. For an operation with antiunitary flag $a_g$, the test is
+$(-1)^{a_g}W_g^{-T}\mathbf k=\mathbf k\pmod{\mathbb Z^3}$. Products are computed modulo integer
+translations of the supplied cell, retaining centering translations as distinct elements. The
+representative of the identity must be the identity rotation with zero translation.
+
+For $g h=T_{L_{g,h}}(gh)$, with $L_{g,h}=\tau_g+W_g\tau_h-\tau_{gh}$ an integer cell translation,
+the factor is
+
+$$\omega(g,h)=z(g,h)\exp[-2\pi i\mathbf k\cdot L_{g,h}].$$
+
+The scalar factor has $z=1$. For spinors, $z=\pm1$ follows from the physical SU(2) lift and, for
+antiunitary operations, $i\sigma_y K$. Thus physical time reversal squares to $-1$, while an
+antiferromagnetic half translation additionally retains its Bloch phase. Associativity is checked
+using the twisted cocycle condition
+
+$$\omega(g,h)\omega(gh,j)
+=\omega(h,j)^{*^{a_g}}\omega(g,hj).$$
+
+Changing coset representatives multiplies the factors by a coboundary. The calculation retains this
+gauge; it does not discard complex nonsymmorphic phases or force spin matrices into an ordinary
+single-valued spatial representation.
+
+For a **unitary** multiplication table and factor system, `projective_characters` generates a
+complete character table directly from the twisted regular representation. Left multiplication acts
+as $L_g|h\rangle=\omega(g,h)|gh\rangle$. Right multiplication $R_g|h\rangle=\omega(h,g)|hg\rangle$
+commutes with every $L_h$. A deterministic Hermitian combination of the right actions splits the
+regular space into invariant irreducible copies using the existing LAPACK eigensolver. Accidental
+unresolved coincidences trigger another combination; failure to obtain a complete table returns an
+error, never partial irreps.
+
+Every accepted table passes invariant-subspace closure, character orthogonality, irreducible-copy
+multiplicities, $\sum_\alpha d_\alpha^2=|G|$, and reconstruction of the regular character.
+Characters use the exact supplied operation order and factor gauge; irrep column order has no
+physical meaning. Conventional-cell centering sectors may include folded translations and are not
+silently identified with primitive-cell EBR labels. Magnetic factor systems can be validated, but
+their antiunitary elements must not be passed to the unitary character generator as if they were
+ordinary group elements.
+
+Analytic tests include a screw with complex Bloch phase, centering, magnetic half translations,
+physical time reversal, representative/origin gauge changes, ordinary $D_2$ and its two-dimensional
+spinor representation. With SPGLIB available, the default unit test covers 14 selected Hall
+settings. `topology_symmetry_unittest --all-settings` covers all 530 settings of the 230 space
+groups at six sampled k-points: Gamma, two half-grid boundary points, a third-grid point, a
+quarter-grid point and a generic point. Scalar/spinor factors are tested for the ordinary groups and
+their grey time-reversal extensions. Character generation is tested only for the unitary groups.
+These are algebra tests, **not** material-level validation or a complete 230-space-group TQC
+classification. The native kernels do not depend on SPGLIB; only database-driven tests do.
+
+An independent local comparison used Spgrep 0.7.0 to decompose the regular representations for all
+6,360 scalar/spinor cases with the same supplied factors. After matching irrep columns, the largest
+character difference was $1.78\times10^{-14}$. Spgrep is a validation reference, not a CP2K runtime
+dependency; agreement on supplied factors does not independently validate the space-group
+identification or the eventual AO band representations.
+
+### Gaussian band representations
+
+The optional [LITTLE_GROUP_IRREPS](#CP2K_INPUT.FORCE_EVAL.DFT.PRINT.WANNIER90.LITTLE_GROUP_IRREPS)
+keyword analyzes the selected bands at explicit `NNKP`, `WILSON` or `TRIM` points. It requires a
+SPGLIB-enabled build, Gaussian basis sets and a three-dimensionally periodic cell. This is an
+analysis of explicitly diagonalized property points, not a new SCF or Wilson-loop symmetry reduction
+mode. In particular, it does not infer a topological classification from an isolated point's irreps.
+
+The symmetry search uses the actual input cell and atomic **kind** labels. All space-group
+operations are retained, including different centering translations with the same rotation. The
+reduced list used for k-point quadrature is not an adequate replacement for this list. For
+
+$$W_g r_A+\tau_g=r_{A'}+L_{A,g},$$
+
+the scalar coefficient action has the nonzero atom block
+
+$$U_g(\mathbf k)_{A',A}=
+e^{-2\pi i\mathbf k\cdot L_{A,g}}D_A(AW_gA^{-1}).$$
+
+Here the columns of $A$ are the cell vectors and $D_A$ uses CP2K's existing contracted Gaussian
+angular rotation. Atomic representatives and Bloch phases use the same cell Fourier gauge as the
+Hamiltonian. Improper rotations keep the orbital parity. SOC adds the conjugate physical SU(2)
+matrix appropriate to CP2K's spin convention. `TIME_REVERSAL T` also adds the grey-group operations
+and checks their antiunitary multiplication laws; it requires a restricted SCF. When antiunitary
+operations preserve the property point, their local corepresentation types and multiplicities are
+also classified as described below. No magnetic-space-group detection or conventional labels are
+implemented.
+
+Each operation must preserve the AO metric, the norm and span of the selected bands, and commute
+with their eigenvalues. The resulting sewing matrices must obey the full projective multiplication
+law, including the nonsymmorphic and spin factors. Unitary irrep multiplicities then follow from
+their characters. No polar projection or gauge fitting is used to hide a failed closure check. The
+selected bands must be separated from computed excluded bands; with SOC, the scalar space used for
+second variation must also be converged and contain complete symmetry multiplets.
+`WANNIER90/ADDED_MOS` controls that additional scalar space. Merely selecting complete final Kramers
+pairs does not repair a truncated scalar multiplet.
+
+`LITTLE_GROUP_EPS_GEOMETRY` is the atom/search tolerance in Bohr; `LITTLE_GROUP_TOLERANCE` checks
+the dimensionless metric, frame and algebra residuals; `LITTLE_GROUP_ENERGY_TOL` bounds the
+eigenvalue commutator in Hartree. Symmetry breaking by a poorly converged SCF, insufficient
+integration grid or incomplete SOC scalar space must be investigated before relaxing these checks.
+The dense reference implementation replicates its matrices on each rank.
+`LITTLE_GROUP_MAX_MEMORY_MB` limits a conservative estimate of additional storage, based on the full
+space-group size before little-group selection; it excludes the pre-existing SCF/export matrices and
+external library workspaces. It is not a sparse/distributed band-representation solver.
+
+`SEED_NAME.little_group` contains a block per k-point:
+
+- `KPOINT` and `SIZES`: fractional reciprocal coordinate, unitary and antiunitary operation counts,
+  number of unitary irreps, and number of selected bands.
+- `OPERATION`: operation index, antiunitary flag, row-major integer direct-space rotation, and
+  fractional translation.
+- `PRODUCT`: two operation indices, their product index, and the complex projective factor.
+- `BAND_CHARACTER`: trace of the selected-band sewing matrix for each unitary operation.
+- `IRREP` and `CHARACTER`: local irrep index, dimension and multiplicity, followed by its character
+  for every unitary element. Complex numbers are real/imaginary pairs.
+- `COREP_SIZES`, `COREP` and `COREP_RESTRICTION`: optional antiunitary classification and unitary
+  restrictions, as described below.
+- `END_KPOINT`: completion of that point's validated decomposition.
+
+Irrep indices are **local**, not Bilbao labels, and their order has no physical meaning. The
+characters and explicit operations must be used when comparing different calculations. Centering
+sectors in nonprimitive cells are retained, not silently relabeled as primitive-cell irreps.
+
+`QS/regtest-little-group` checks the scalar $s+p$ and SOC $s_{1/2}+p_{1/2}+p_{3/2}$ occupied Ne
+multiplets. It also tests two occupied He bands at $k_z=1/4$ with a half-cell translation, an
+equivalent sheared cell, and a twofold screw. The latter operations square to a translation with
+phase $-i$, rather than to the identity. A Ne/SOC screw test combines that phase with the spin minus
+sign, giving $+i$. The He screw is also tested with all-electron GAPW and a UZH TZVPP basis with
+contracted s/p/d functions. These small Gaussian calculations exercise the real AO-to-band path in
+addition to the all-setting algebra tests; they are not material-level validation of every space
+group or a complete TQC implementation. An independent comparison of the Ne Gamma-point band
+characters against the analytic occupied $s+p$ representation and its spinful product gave a maximum
+difference of $7.2\times10^{-15}$ in serial and two-rank MPI runs. Negative local tests cut a
+degenerate scalar multiplet or truncate the scalar SOC space; both abort before accepting irrep
+multiplicities. The sheared-cell smoke uses a tighter integration grid and SCF threshold, rather
+than relaxing the representation tolerance.
+
+### Antiunitary corepresentations
+
+With `LITTLE_GROUP_IRREPS T` and `TIME_REVERSAL T`, no additional input switch is needed. At a point
+whose little group contains antiunitary operations, CP2K classifies its irreducible
+corepresentations. A combined operation such as inversion times time reversal may preserve a generic
+k-point even when time reversal alone does not. At points without an antiunitary stabilizer, only
+the unitary table is reported. The existing restricted-SCF, subspace and sewing-product checks
+remain mandatory.
+
+Write the little group as $M=H\cup aH$, with $H$ unitary and $a$ antiunitary. For a unitary irrep
+$\alpha$, CP2K evaluates the Wigner test and its conjugate character in the actual factor gauge:
+
+$$w_\alpha=\frac{1}{|H|}\sum_{b\in aH}\omega(b,b)\chi_\alpha(b^2),$$
+
+$$\chi_\alpha^a(h)=
+\frac{\omega(h,a)}{\omega(a,a^{-1}ha)}\chi_\alpha(a^{-1}ha)^*.$$
+
+The indicator must be real and within tolerance of $+1$, $-1$ or $0$. These cases respectively
+retain $\alpha$, require two copies of $\alpha$, or pair $\alpha$ with an inequivalent conjugate
+irrep. Thus a type $-1$ unitary multiplicity must be even, while type $0$ partners must have equal
+multiplicities. The resulting integer restriction matrix converts between unitary and
+corepresentation band counts. The formulation follows the projective antiunitary conventions in the
+[Spgrep formulation notes](https://spglib.github.io/spgrep/formulation/irreps/corep.html); CP2K
+implements the character and integer-count operations natively.
+
+These signs are representation types, **not topological indices** or eigenvalues of an antiunitary
+operator. In particular, type $+1$ does not rule out Kramers degeneracy: a multidimensional unitary
+spin irrep may already contain the required pair. Conversely, at a generic centrosymmetric SOC point
+with only the identity in $H$, the type $-1$ restriction supplies the two-dimensional pair. Spin
+signs and nonsymmorphic phases both enter $\omega(b,b)$ and must not be omitted.
+
+`COREP_SIZES` gives the number of local irreducible corepresentations. Each `COREP` record contains
+its index, dimension, Wigner indicator and selected-band multiplicity. `COREP_RESTRICTION` contains
+the corepresentation index, unitary irrep index and multiplicity in that restriction. The complete
+table includes corepresentations absent from the selected bands. No gauge-dependent trace is
+assigned to an antiunitary matrix. CP2K does not rotate the eigenvectors to a canonical
+corepresentation basis; it classifies the already validated physical band sewing matrices. Character
+pairing, integer counts and spinor phases are not repaired when checks fail.
+
+The native kernel accepts any validated finite antiunitary factor system. The electronic-structure
+input currently generates the nonmagnetic grey extension of the spatial symmetry group; this is not
+a magnetic-space-group search or support for arbitrary noncollinear magnetic ground states. Nor does
+it construct a complete Brillouin-zone compatibility graph or a general EBR classification.
+
+Analytic tests include a scalar/spinful magnetic half translation at Gamma and the zone boundary, an
+already complete quaternion spin doublet, a complex $C_3$ partner pair, complex twisted gauge
+changes, reordered operations/irreps, and rejection of odd or unpaired counts. The 530-Hall-setting
+suite tests 5,324 antiunitary little-group tables; the remaining sampled grey-group cases have no
+antiunitary stabilizer. Ne Gaussian tests exercise all three types: scalar and SOC multiplets at
+Gamma, inversion/time-reversal pairs at a generic point, and paired screw sectors with SOC.
+
+An additional local algebra sweep used SPGLIB 2.7's magnetic database in its supplied setting for
+each of the 1,651 UNI numbers, at the same six k-points with scalar and spinful factors. All 19,812
+factor systems passed; 14,088 had an antiunitary stabilizer and yielded a corepresentation table.
+The other 5,724 cases contained only unitary little-group operations. This tests supplied magnetic
+group algebra, not magnetic electronic-structure calculations or all alternative cell settings.
+
+For the 10,154 primitive-cell tables supported by the independent Spgrep 0.7.0 comparison, Wigner
+types, dimensions and unitary restrictions agreed, with a largest character residual of
+$7.11\times10^{-15}$. This comparison uses the same supplied factors and therefore does not
+independently validate symmetry detection. It also does not certify Spgrep's explicit antiunitary
+matrices: a separate product check in that supplied-factor comparison did not pass for all such
+matrices. None are imported into CP2K. The physical CP2K sewing matrices are tested directly against
+the full semilinear product law before their corepresentation counts are accepted.
+
+### Band compatibility along explicit segments
+
+Enable
+[LITTLE_GROUP_COMPATIBILITY](#CP2K_INPUT.FORCE_EVAL.DFT.PRINT.WANNIER90.LITTLE_GROUP_COMPATIBILITY)
+to compare selected-band representations along the directed links of `KPOINTS_SOURCE NNKP` or
+`WILSON`; `SYMMETRY` enables it automatically for its generated paths. This implies
+`LITTLE_GROUP_IRREPS` and inherits its Gaussian-basis, SPGLIB, cell, subspace-isolation and
+covariance requirements. `TRIM` alone is insufficient because it does not define segments. The
+analysis does not change SCF weights or Wilson connection matrices.
+
+For a link from $\mathbf k_a$ to the target image $\mathbf k_b+\mathbf G$, define
+$\mathbf d=\mathbf k_b+\mathbf G-\mathbf k_a$. The segment subgroup contains the endpoint operations
+satisfying $\sigma_g W_g^T\mathbf d=\mathbf d$, with $\sigma_g=+1$ for unitary and $-1$ for
+antiunitary operations, exactly within the fractional-coordinate tolerance, **not** merely modulo a
+reciprocal vector. Intersecting the two endpoint little groups is not enough: an operation may
+preserve both endpoints but not their connecting line. Distinct centering translations are retained.
+Operations at the two endpoints are matched by their antiunitary flag, rotation and translation
+modulo integer direct-cell translations.
+
+Before restricting the representations, remove their continuously varying translational phase:
+
+$$\eta_g(\mathbf k)=e^{2\pi i\mathbf k\cdot\tau_g},\qquad
+\bar\chi_g(\mathbf k)=\eta_g(\mathbf k)\chi_g(\mathbf k),$$
+
+$$\bar\omega(g,h)=\eta_g\eta_h^{\sigma_g}\eta_{gh}^*\omega(g,h).$$
+
+Here $\eta_h^{-1}=\eta_h^*$ because the phases have unit modulus. The complex conjugation for an
+antiunitary first factor is essential; applying the ordinary unitary rephasing would generally
+produce different endpoint factors even on a symmetry-preserving line.
+
+The two endpoints must give the same rephased factor on the segment subgroup. Its projective
+character table defines a common gauge for the restrictions. If $B_a$ and $B_b$ are the resulting
+integer branching matrices and $m_a,m_b$ the endpoint irrep multiplicities, the necessary
+compatibility condition is $B_a m_a=B_b m_b$. This compares representation subspaces, not individual
+eigenvector phases or an arbitrary ordering of irrep columns.
+
+If an endpoint has antiunitary symmetry, its complete corepresentations are restricted through their
+unitary characters. If the line also has an antiunitary coset, the Wigner table of the common line
+factor determines its irreducible corepresentations. Writing the endpoint and line unitary
+restriction matrices as $R_a$ and $R_\ell$, the integer corepresentation branching $C_a$ satisfies
+$R_\ell C_a=B_a R_a$. Pairing and doubling constraints are checked exactly, not rounded. If the line
+has no antiunitary symmetry, $B_a R_a$ gives the branching into its ordinary projective irreps. Thus
+endpoint Kramers partners remain paired in the connectivity data even if time reversal does not fix
+the interior. For a spinful glide, opposite partners at Gamma can become same-branch pairs at a zone
+boundary, enforcing a four-band connection rather than an isolated two-band pair.
+
+For validated endpoint corepresentations, equality of line unitary counts is equivalent to equality
+of line corepresentation counts. The additional output resolves the allowed endpoint-to-line
+pairing; it is not an independent topological invariant or a test of the spectrum between samples.
+
+The destination phase uses $\mathbf k_b+\mathbf G$, although the band data are stored at
+$\mathbf k_b$. Discarding $\mathbf G$ would lose nonsymmorphic monodromy. For example, the two
+eigenvalue branches of a twofold screw exchange after one reciprocal period along its axis. A single
+branch can be isolated at a sampled point but cannot form a closed isolated band over that loop.
+Integer changes of operation representatives cancel between their characters and the rephasing; they
+must not change the compatibility result.
+
+After the point records, `SEED_NAME.little_group` contains:
+
+- `EDGE`: source point, target point and its three reciprocal-image integers. Point indices are
+  one-based in the explicit NNKP or generated Wilson/SYMMETRY order; zero-length links are omitted.
+- `LINE_MEMBER`: line-operation index and its operation indices at the two endpoints.
+- `LINE_CHARACTER`: line irrep and operation indices, followed by real/imaginary character parts.
+- `LEFT_RESTRICTION` and `RIGHT_RESTRICTION`: endpoint irrep, line irrep and branching multiplicity.
+- `LINE_MULTIPLICITY`: line irrep and the two selected-band counts, including zero counts.
+- `LINE_ANTIUNITARY_MEMBER`: antiunitary line-member index and its operation indices at both
+  endpoints.
+- `LINE_COREP`: line corepresentation index, dimension, Wigner type, and left/right selected counts.
+- `LINE_COREP_RESTRICTION`: line corepresentation, unitary line irrep, and restriction multiplicity.
+- `LEFT_COREP_RESTRICTION` and `RIGHT_COREP_RESTRICTION`: endpoint corepresentation index, line
+  representation index and branching multiplicity. The line index refers to `LINE_COREP` when that
+  table is present and to `LINE_CHARACTER` otherwise. Endpoint indices match the point's `COREP`
+  records. These records are absent for an endpoint without antiunitary symmetry.
+- `END_EDGE`: completion of the comparison.
+
+The summary reports the number of checked and incompatible directed segments and the number with an
+antiunitary line stabilizer. Incompatible counts are a physical diagnostic to investigate band
+selection, symmetry and gap closures, not a reason to modify the characters or abort a valid algebra
+calculation. Failed factors or noninteger restrictions do abort. Compatible endpoint counts do
+**not** prove a gap along the segment, a trivial band structure or an atomic-band decomposition.
+Only the requested or generated segments are examined; a complete equivariant connectivity
+classification and EBR elementarity remain separate tasks.
+
+Only operations, factors, characters and multiplicities are retained between points, not the AO
+actions or band sewing matrices. The retained data contribute to `LITTLE_GROUP_MAX_MEMORY_MB`, in
+addition to the temporary point and edge workspaces. AO/spinor actions and first-factor rows of the
+sewing-product check are distributed over the complete property MPI environment. Directed segment
+algebra is evaluated in bounded batches: a rank retains at most one owned edge and one received
+edge, rather than a whole batch or graph of edge results. Zero-length links and idle ranks are
+included in the ownership checks; failures are propagated collectively before data delivery. Edge
+records are delivered in the original graph order, preserving reciprocal-image phases and optional
+antiunitary endpoint/line data.
+
+This is not a fully distributed-memory TQC implementation: the AO metric, selected frames, small
+representation tables and retained point data remain replicated. Character/corepresentation and
+atomic-signature lattice calculations are also still replicated. Local serial/MPI agreement does not
+establish multi-node scaling.
+
+Analytic unit tests check scalar/spinful screw branches, reciprocal monodromy, integer changes of
+coset representatives, arbitrary irrep ordering, incompatible factors and endpoint symmetries that
+do not fix the line. A $C_{4v}$ doublet must split into one even and one odd mirror branch;
+replacing these by two copies of one branch is incompatible despite the equal rank. The all-setting
+test adds 12,720 ordinary/grey segment comparisons over all 530 Hall settings, including 4,462 with
+antiunitary line stabilizers, using complete generic-site band orbits and both scalar and
+physical-spin multiplicities. The database tests check algebra, not a material survey. Gaussian
+regtests close a full reciprocal loop for scalar He/GPW, He/GAPW and Ne/SOC, including a shifted
+target image. A separate one-band He test must report the screw monodromy obstruction. An automatic
+Wilson-surface test exercises the generated links and their repeated refinement. A five-segment He
+loop without time reversal also includes zero-length links and incomplete MPI batches, checking the
+unitary-only data path.
+
+Antiunitary tests additionally cover shifted-origin PT pairs at generic points, time reversal
+present only at endpoints, magnetic-glide Kramers planes, rejected odd endpoint multiplicities, and
+the two-band obstruction versus four-band compatibility of a spinful glide/hourglass model.
+
+## Site-induced atomic band characters
+
+The native `topology_band_representations` module constructs atomic band characters from a supplied
+site and a complete group modulo integer translations. This is the reference-data layer for the
+sampled atomic-band comparison described below, not a complete EBR classifier. It preserves the
+supplied cell and operation convention, including centering translations.
+
+`atomic_band_construct` builds the site's full orbit and stabilizer. For a unitary stabilizer, the
+columns correspond to local projective irreps. For an antiunitary stabilizer they instead correspond
+to complete onsite corepresentations, with the required partner pairing or doubling. The site
+operations are adjusted by integer translations to fix the seed exactly, so their local factor is
+the spin factor at Gamma, not a k-dependent nonsymmorphic factor.
+
+`atomic_band_characters` evaluates those same columns at arbitrary reciprocal points. Only orbit
+sites fixed modulo integer translations contribute to a unitary character. Writing the orbit
+representative as $t_j$, the displacement as $L_{gj}=W_gq_j+\tau_g-q_j$, and the exact site
+operation as $h_j=t_j^{-1}\{I|-L_{gj}\}g t_j$, its contribution is
+
+$$e^{-2\pi i\mathbf k\cdot L_{gj}}\,
+\frac{z(g,t_j)}{z(t_j,h_j)}\,{}^{a_{t_j}}\chi(h_j).$$
+
+Here $z$ is the spin factor and an antiunitary $t_j$ conjugates the local character. This
+conjugation is needed even for a unitary target operation. The API returns original input operation
+indices with every character row. `atomic_band_site_data` exposes the orbit positions and onsite
+unitary characters, allowing physical orbital content to be matched without assigning arbitrary
+eigenvector labels. Invalid geometry or a nonclosed group leaves an empty result.
+
+The band rank is the number of orbit sites times the local dimension. An induced band need not be
+elementary, and equivalent characters at sampled points do not establish equivalent Bloch bundles.
+The separate geometry kernel below supplies Wyckoff representatives. Elementarity,
+conventional-to-primitive conversion and a complete atomic-band catalogue remain separate
+requirements for general TQC classification.
+
+`topology_band_unittest --all-settings` covers all 530 Hall settings, three supplied positions, six
+k points, scalar/spinful factors, and ordinary/grey groups. Every induced column is decomposed into
+native little-group irreps, checked against antiunitary pairing, and restricted along six directed
+connections including a reciprocal-image loop. Independently computed Cartesian s/p/d orbital
+traces, multiplied by physical spin traces, match the corresponding induced sums. Analytic cases
+include the eight inversion centers, nonsymmorphic screw orbits, quaternion doublets, antiunitary
+orbit representatives, and interleaved operation ordering. These checks validate the induction
+kernel; they neither enumerate all Wyckoff positions nor classify materials.
+
+## Generated Wyckoff families
+
+`topology_wyckoff:wyckoff_families` generates the site-symmetry families of the supplied group,
+including unoccupied positions. It does not infer candidate sites from the actual atoms or a
+coordinate grid. The input is the same complete group modulo cell translations used for site
+induction, with an arbitrary cell, origin, operation ordering and integer coset convention.
+
+For each spatial operation it solves $(W-I)x=-\tau\pmod{\mathbb Z^3}$ and retains all connected
+components and nonempty intersections. Integer row-lattice saturation distinguishes, for example,
+the two components of $2x=0\pmod1$. A family closure is stored as $Bx=c\pmod{\mathbb Z^r}$, with
+primitive integer normals and dimension $3-r$. Integer right-hand sides are bounded using the unit
+cube, not a guessed coordinate denominator. Particular solutions outside that cube are equivalent on
+the torus. Space-group-equivalent families are identified only after intersection closure.
+
+Each family has a generic representative verified against its complete stabilizer, an orbit
+multiplicity, and indices of all higher-symmetry families in its closure. These specialization
+relations identify maximal site groups. They do **not** prove that every induced representation from
+such a site is elementary. Output has no standard Wyckoff letters. Numerical instability or an
+arithmetic/work limit returns an error and no partial catalogue. Antiunitary operations act on
+coordinates through their spatial parts but remain distinguished in the reported stabilizers.
+
+`topology_wyckoff_unittest --all-settings` constructs 3,467 families over all 530 ordinary Hall
+settings. Independent spinful induction checks each orbit multiplicity and site stabilizer. External
+validation matches every family bijectively to SPGLIB's tabulated `Wyckoff.csv` affine coordinates
+and checks 5,648 directed specialization relations. The production kernel does not read that table.
+Combined arbitrary origin, nonorthogonal unimodular cell, integer coset and operation-order changes
+preserve the family and specialization graph. Analytic tests also check grey groups, a magnetic half
+translation, screw axes with no fixed sites, and rejected nonclosed input groups. This is a
+geometric and representation validation, not a magnetic material survey or a completed EBR
+classifier.
+
+## Atomic signature matrices on explicit reciprocal sets
+
+The native `topology_atomic_signatures` module combines generated Wyckoff families, onsite induction
+and the little-group tables into one integer matrix for supplied reciprocal points. This is a
+reference-construction API used by `ATOMIC_SIGNATURES`, not a classification claim.
+`atomic_signature_construct` retains every site and its onsite irrep/corepresentation as a separate
+column. It includes nonmaximal sites and does not merge columns that happen to coincide at the
+sampled points. Equivalent sampled symmetry data need not imply equivalent band bundles.
+
+The separate `character_class` diagnostic compares complete **unitary character functions** on every
+symmetry-invariant reciprocal torus, not just the supplied points. For a direct-space integer
+rotation $W$, the torus is $K_W=\{k:(W^T-I)k\in\mathbb Z^3\}$ and its character lattice is
+$\mathbb Z^3/(W-I)\mathbb Z^3$. The checked Smith factorization $U(W-I)V=D$ collects the finite
+Bloch terms by the keys $UL$, reduced modulo the nonzero Smith divisors; zero-divisor rows remain
+free integer coordinates. Comparing these collected coefficients includes disconnected torsion
+components. Coefficients are compared at the declared tolerance after scaling by band dimension; the
+lattice operations themselves use checked integer arithmetic.
+
+`ATOMIC_CHARACTER_CLASS column representative` uses the first matching global column as a local
+class identifier. `ATOMIC_CHARACTER_CLASS_RESIDUAL` reports the largest accepted coefficient
+residual. Identifiers are not canonical labels. Antiunitary orbit representatives conjugate local
+characters as in induction, but antiunitary operators have no basis-independent trace and are not
+assigned Fourier characters. Overlapping nontransitive tolerance neighborhoods are rejected. This
+diagnostic does **not** merge columns, change the sampled membership problem or supply
+site-intertwining/homotopy witnesses. Even complete character equality is not a proof of
+Bloch-bundle equivalence, connectivity or topological triviality.
+
+Rows are grouped by the supplied k-point order. A point with an antiunitary little-group coset uses
+full corepresentation multiplicities; other points use unitary irrep multiplicities. `row_begin` and
+`row_dimension` define each block, while `column_site`, `column_local` and `band_dimension` retain
+provenance and physical band rank. Every column has the same rank in all blocks after multiplication
+by the row dimensions. At an antiunitary point, partner pairing and doubling are checked before any
+counts are accepted. These are not counts of Kramers pairs unless the particular corepresentations
+actually have that dimension.
+
+`atomic_signature_decompose` maps an incoming unitary character vector, in any row order, to the
+same point-specific signature basis. Operation indices refer to the original full list used by the
+constructor. The spin/Bloch gauge must be identical. Missing or duplicated operations, noninteger
+character decomposition, or violated antiunitary pairing return an error and invalid counts. This
+does not replace the physical AO/subspace/Hamiltonian covariance checks required when the vector
+comes from electronic-structure data. The stored `little_group_data_type` contains zero
+selected-band counts: it supplies tables for subduction, not observed bands.
+
+The matrix is a potentially redundant atomic signature matrix for the **explicit reciprocal
+sample**. Neither a complete set of Brillouin-zone strata nor representation elementarity is
+inferred. Conventional-cell translation sectors remain explicit. The integer kernel below tests
+membership and nonnegative decomposition in this supplied matrix. Elementarity and physical
+topological classification remain separate tasks.
+
+The catalogue retains a compact onsite snapshot for every site, copied from the same construction
+that produced its signature columns. The Gaussian detail file includes `ATOMIC_ONSITE_SIZES` (site,
+full and unitary operation counts, local column count, spinful flag), `ATOMIC_ONSITE_OPERATION`
+(site, local operation, original full-group operation, antiunitary flag, row-major Cartesian
+rotation and the three lattice shifts), `ATOMIC_ONSITE_SPIN` (site, operation, column-major complex
+spin matrix), `ATOMIC_ONSITE_PRODUCT_ROW` (site, left operation and signed product indices for all
+right operations), and `ATOMIC_ONSITE_CHARACTER` (site, local column, local unitary operation and
+complex character). Complex entries are written as real/imaginary pairs. The existing
+`ATOMIC_SIGNATURE_COLUMN` records connect these local columns to the induced band columns.
+`ATOMIC_REFERENCE_SPIN` additionally stores the physical spin lift of every original full-group
+operation in column-major order, including those without a fixed site. The lifts come from the same
+factor-system construction, not a second independently signed quaternion conversion. They describe
+the spin-1/2 action even for a scalar catalogue, where the spinful flag is zero and these matrices
+must not be applied to scalar orbital characters; scalar onsite factors are one. The signed product
+index encodes both the absolute product-operation index and the exactly real factor $+1$ or $-1$.
+The writer checks this property before using the compact format; no character or factor is rounded
+to reduce the output size.
+
+The shift is defined by $W_gq+\tau_g=q+L_g$, with the seed reduced modulo the input lattice. Onsite
+characters refer to the operation fixing $q$ exactly; its factor contains spin and antiunitary
+phases, not the Bloch phase of the original translation representative. Induction must restore the
+lattice phase $\exp(-2\pi i k\cdot L_g)$ and transport the onsite representation to the other orbit
+sites. The retained spin lifts include physical time reversal for spinful antiunitary operations.
+These records preserve the convention needed for independent induction and external label matching;
+they do not assign canonical site/EBR labels from a dimension or a sampled band signature. No
+reference database is required at runtime. The additional retained arrays are included in the memory
+estimate.
+
+`topology_atomic_unittest --all-settings` constructs matrices for all 530 Hall settings, scalar and
+spinful factors, ordinary and grey groups, and six supplied reciprocal points. It checks all column
+ranks, directed segment subductions and a reciprocal-image loop, plus additivity and permuted
+character-row reconstruction. Analytic tests recover the sixteen inversion-center signatures and the
+generic-site signature, with and without Kramers pairing. Integer changes of coset representatives
+give the required Bloch rephasing, not arbitrary column changes. The regular CI sample includes
+primitive, centered, screw and complex-C3 cases and remains bounded.
+
+## Real-space induction and composite witnesses
+
+`ATOMIC_SIGNATURES` also constructs representation-level witnesses for every generated Wyckoff
+specialization. `topology_site_relations` places an image of the destination in the source's lifted
+affine closure using an integer-lattice solve. Every source stabilizer must fix the entire straight
+displacement, not just its endpoints modulo lattice translations. Finite site-group induction then
+gives a nonnegative integer matrix $B$ such that
+
+$$
+\operatorname{BR}_{q,\alpha}
+\simeq \bigoplus_\beta B_{\beta\alpha}\operatorname{BR}_{q',\beta}.
+$$
+
+The construction retains physical spin factors and onsite antiunitary corepresentations. Their
+unitary character norms need not be one; the decomposition checks both the full character and
+dimension. Destination orbit images retain the canonical seed's representation-column labels. This
+is an induction/homotopy witness, not an identification based on equal sampled k characters.
+
+Singleton induction relations are composed into equivalence classes, preferring maximal-site
+representatives. A multi-summand relation proves that its source class is composite. Recursive
+expansion terminates because every nonzero summand has strictly smaller positive band dimension. The
+nonnegative expansion $E$ is checked against the independently generated signature matrix: $AE=A$,
+with conserved physical dimensions. Original columns and signatures are retained.
+
+The detailed output contains `SITE_INDUCTION_EDGE` (source/destination, operation, integer lift and
+endpoint), `SITE_INDUCTION_ENTRY` (local branching multiplicities), `SITE_INDUCTION_COLUMN`
+(equivalent representative and decomposition provenance), and `SITE_INDUCTION_EXPANSION` (global
+nonzero expansion entries). An absent composite witness is not automatically a certificate of EBR
+elementarity, complete reciprocal connectivity, or triviality of the selected Gaussian bands.
+
+The native `topology_site_unittest --all-settings` validates 22,592 specialization edges for all 530
+Hall settings in ordinary/grey scalar/spinful variants, including 180,736 reciprocal character
+checks. Additional analytic cases cover lifted paths, changed cell/origin/coset conventions and
+malformed witnesses. The bounded CI subset includes the spinless time-reversal exception at an $S_4$
+site in space group 84. Development comparisons reproduce the space-group coverage of the exception
+tables of Cano et al. and the maximal-site generator counts in all 2,120 variants against the Bilbao
+EBR tables archived by Crystalline.jl. Count agreement is not a canonical-irrep-by-irrep
+identification.
+
+### Independent affine-reference matching
+
+`wyckoff_match_affine` matches a supplied complete reference catalogue to the generated site
+families. For a reference $q=q_0+D\alpha$ and a generated closure $Bq=c$ modulo integers, it checks
+the rank of $D$, the exact integer identity $B W_g D=0$, the fractional offset $B(W_gq_0+\tau_g)-c$,
+and the orbit multiplicity. The result must be a bijection. This compares the whole family: an
+origin lying on a higher-symmetry subset cannot substitute for its free tangent space. Duplicate
+entries, missing matches and malformed inputs return no partial mapping.
+
+The reference must already use the supplied cell and origin convention. Labels remain metadata; the
+routine does not infer standard letters from a generic coordinate or assign canonical onsite irrep
+names. `topology_site_unittest --reference=FILE` accepts an independent numeric fixture for
+development comparisons, without using the SPGLIB operation database in this reference path. The
+fixture starts with a group count. Each group supplies its identifier, full operation count and
+family count, then rotations/translations and label/multiplicity/origin/direction records.
+Three-by-three matrices use Fortran column order. No reference table is installed at runtime.
+
+The independently archived generators and affine positions for all 230 space groups give 1,731
+families, or 6,924 matches across ordinary/grey scalar/spinful variants. Combined cell shear, origin
+shifts, integer coset changes and reversed operation order preserve the named specialization graphs.
+The dimensions of all 10,398 unreduced maximal-site generators match the reference EBR dimension
+multisets **separately at every Wyckoff position**, with the conventional-cell centering factor
+removed for comparison. This is stronger than matching total counts, but does not identify distinct
+equal-dimensional onsite irreps or establish full EBR character equivalence.
+
+## Exact integer decomposition of supplied signatures
+
+`topology_integer_lattice` accepts integer matrices independently of their representation-theory
+origin. `integer_lattice_factor` constructs the Smith form $UAV=D$ using checked 64-bit Euclidean
+row and column operations. The transformations are unimodular, diagonal entries are positive in
+divisibility order, and the full certificate is multiplied out before acceptance. Rectangular and
+rank-deficient matrices are allowed. `integer_lattice_certificate` exposes the transforms, diagonal
+and rank without mutable access to the factorization.
+
+For a signature vector $b$, `integer_lattice_solve` tests whether $Ub$ is divisible by the nonzero
+diagonal entries and zero in the remaining rows. It returns a signed integer witness $x$ and
+verifies $Ax=b$. Nonmembership is an exact result only when the status is zero. Coefficient growth
+or a work limit is an **unresolved** result, never a topological obstruction. No floating-point rank
+threshold, linear-program relaxation or coefficient rounding is used for these decisions.
+
+`nonnegative_integer_solve` separately tests $Ax=b$ with $x\geq0$ for nonnegative $A,b$. It searches
+bounded integer coefficients, using componentwise bounds, suffix gcds and zero-support pruning. Zero
+columns can be assigned zero. A returned witness is checked again. Only a completed search can prove
+that no nonnegative decomposition exists; exhaustion of the caller's mandatory node budget returns
+status 1, explicitly undecided. The search can be exponential and is not a large-catalogue
+integer-optimization backend.
+
+Both decisions refer only to the supplied signature matrix. Signed membership does not imply
+nonnegative membership, and nonnegative membership does not by itself establish a topologically
+trivial Bloch bundle. In particular, Smith divisors of rows at an incomplete reciprocal sample are
+not automatically the space group's symmetry-indicator group. Complete reciprocal strata,
+compatibility constraints, band isolation, and the interpretation/equivalence of atomic columns
+remain distinct requirements. The integer kernel is independent of physical band validation; the
+Gaussian integration below supplies the latter separately.
+
+`topology_integer_unittest` independently checks Smith invariants against gcds of every minor and
+unimodularity for 384 small matrices of ranks zero through four. All 6,642 two-pair inversion
+patterns at four/eight TRIM agree with the analytic Fourier criterion, including signed-only cases.
+A further 18,225 small nonnegative systems agree with exhaustive coefficient enumeration. Invalid
+input, arithmetic growth and exhausted budgets are tested separately. `topology_atomic_unittest`
+additionally factors the actual generated catalogues and reconstructs signed targets, every atomic
+column, and a three-column sum; the all-setting run covers 2,120 matrices and 33,199 nonnegative
+witnesses.
+
+## Comparing actual Gaussian-band signatures
+
+### Joint compatibility lattice
+
+With compatibility analysis enabled, `ATOMIC_SIGNATURES` also assembles all supplied directed-edge
+branching equations into one integer matrix $C$. Endpoint coordinates are the same unitary-irrep or
+full-corepresentation multiplicities as in the atomic matrix $A$. Corepresentation restrictions are
+included before assembling the unitary line equations; valid line corepresentation counts are
+uniquely determined by their unitary restrictions. A self-edge with a reciprocal shift contributes
+both endpoint restrictions to the **same** columns, retaining screw/glide monodromy. Sign-equivalent
+duplicate rows are removed, not rationally independent integer constraints.
+
+`topology_star_transport` additionally identifies symmetry-related supplied points. For an operation
+$s$ taking $k_a$ to $k_b+G$, let $h_a=s^{-1}hs$ and $L=\tau_h+W_h\tau_s-\tau_s-W_s\tau_{h_a}$. The
+transported unitary character is
+
+$$
+\chi_b(h)=\frac{z(h,s)}{z(s,h_a)}e^{-2\pi i k_b\cdot L}
+\begin{cases}\chi_a(h_a),&s\text{ unitary},\\
+\chi_a(h_a)^*,&s\text{ antiunitary}.\end{cases}
+$$
+
+Here $z$ is the Gamma spin factor, including physical time-reversal squaring. The implementation
+checks conjugation of the complete product table and the twisted factor law, not only character
+inner products. The resulting unitary-irrep map must be a permutation. Antiunitary corepresentation
+coordinates are reconstructed from their unitary restrictions and must also map bijectively,
+preserving dimensions. Independently changed endpoint lattice representatives retain their Bloch
+phases. A spanning set of star equations $M_s n_a-n_b=0$ joins the directed-edge constraints in $C$;
+reciprocal winding of an edge is still a separate constraint.
+
+`topology_compatibility_lattice` computes a saturated integer kernel $K$ of $C$ using the unimodular
+right transform of its Smith form. It then solves $A=KY$ exactly and factors $Y$:
+
+$$
+\mathcal B_G=\ker_{\mathbb Z} C,\qquad
+\mathcal X_G=\mathcal B_G/\operatorname{im}_{\mathbb Z} A
+            \simeq \mathbb Z^{q-r}\oplus\bigoplus_{i=1}^{r}\mathbb Z_{d_i}.
+$$
+
+Here $G$ denotes the supplied graph, $q$ its compatible signature rank and $r$ the rank of $Y$. A
+divisor $d_i=1$ is trivial; stored zeros denote free integer factors. For a compatible target
+$b=Kz$, the Smith row transform $U_Yz$ supplies residues modulo $d_i$ and signed free coordinates.
+All coordinates vanish exactly when $b$ has a **signed** atomic decomposition. An incompatible
+target has no quotient class. This separates compatibility failure from a nonzero compatible class;
+neither arithmetic nor memory exhaustion is interpreted as either result.
+
+The detailed output adds `COMPATIBILITY_CONSTRAINT`, `COMPATIBILITY_KERNEL`,
+`COMPATIBILITY_ATOMIC_COORDINATE`, `COMPATIBILITY_QUOTIENT_MAP`, `COMPATIBILITY_QUOTIENT_FACTOR`
+and, for compatible targets, `COMPATIBILITY_QUOTIENT_CLASS` records. Indices refer to the
+atomic-signature row basis printed in the same file. Matrix records contain nonzero entries; sizes
+are explicit. They permit independent checks of $CK=0$, $KY=A$, kernel saturation and quotient
+residues. A zero constraint row represents a graph with no nonzero equations. `COMPATIBILITY_STAR`
+records give source/destination point, the original full-group operation index, and the three
+reciprocal-shift integers. `Sampled star identifications` counts these spanning links.
+
+This is **not automatically the full space-group symmetry-indicator group**. Star-arm
+identifications between the supplied points are included, but missing points or global compatibility
+constraints can still leave free factors. Even a finite quotient does not certify missing
+reciprocal-stratum coverage, EBR completeness or BZ-wide band isolation. No free factor is labelled
+a physical topological invariant. Signed/nonnegative atomic membership and the physical validation
+described below remain separate.
+
+Integer tests recover the inversion quotients $\mathbb Z_2$ and $\mathbb Z_2^3\times\mathbb Z_4$
+from explicit four/eight-point parity columns and equal-rank constraints, and compare every two-pair
+signature with direct signed membership. Native graph tests check all induced atomic columns,
+reversed edges, reciprocal winding, antiunitary restrictions, incompatible reference columns and
+memory limits. Gaussian tests additionally check GPW/GAPW, SOC, sheared cells, partial graphs with
+free factors and the incompatible one-band screw loop. Star tests check inverse maps, all
+alternative operations between each retained pair, independently induced atomic columns, a complex
+threefold screw and changed endpoint representatives. Invalid factors and numerically unsafe phases
+must fail without returning a coordinate map. The full development sweep reproduces both compatible
+ranks and finite invariant factors in all 2,120 Hall-setting/symmetry combinations when compared
+independently with Tables I-IV and IX-XII of
+[Po, Vishwanath and Watanabe (2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.00911). This benchmark does not
+prove completeness of an arbitrary supplied graph or elementary-band labels.
+
+### Gaussian integration
+
+Enable `ATOMIC_SIGNATURES T` in `DFT/PRINT/WANNIER90` with explicit `NNKP`, `WILSON` or `TRIM`
+points, or automatic `SYMMETRY` sampling. It implies `LITTLE_GROUP_IRREPS` and requires SPGLIB. For
+example, add to an existing NNKP setup with a selected, isolated band set:
+
+```text
+ATOMIC_SIGNATURES T
+ATOMIC_SIGNATURE_MAX_NODES 1000000
+LITTLE_GROUP_COMPATIBILITY T
+```
+
+The compatibility option is recommended for the NNKP/Wilson neighbour segments and adds the joint
+lattice above; it is not required for a TRIM-only signature. All existing physical guards remain:
+the cell must be periodic in all three directions; the selected/excluded bands must have a gap at
+each sampled point; metric covariance, selected-subspace closure, eigenvalue commutation and sewing
+products must pass. SOC uses the existing post-SCF GTH treatment. Time reversal requires a
+restricted SCF. The selected set need not be an occupied prefix, but an insulating ground-state
+classification requires the full occupied space and separately established BZ-wide isolation.
+
+The code generates all site-induced columns in the actual input-cell setting and matches the
+validated Gaussian little groups bijectively to the reference operations. Operation ordering and
+local irrep ordering do not define identity. When a reference operation differs by an integer
+translation $L$, its band character is rephased by $e^{-2\pi i k\cdot L}$. The full product and
+factor tables, including antiunitary conjugation, must transform consistently before decomposition.
+Different cells, mismatched operations, inconsistent spin factors or different band ranks are
+rejected. This is an algebraic convention conversion, not a fitted gauge rotation.
+
+The summary reports two `Sampled ... atomic membership` values: `1` means yes, `0` means a proved
+negative decision for the supplied matrix, and `-1` means unresolved. `ATOMIC_SIGNATURE_MAX_NODES`
+defaults to one million; zero skips the nonnegative search. Exact signed nonmembership also proves
+nonnegative nonmembership without a search. Arithmetic-growth limits can leave signed membership
+unresolved; a verified nonnegative witness still proves signed membership. Invalid data or a failed
+witness check abort rather than reporting a physical conclusion. Reference construction and solver
+matrices use the estimated `LITTLE_GROUP_MAX_MEMORY_MB` budget; the geometric site search and
+external-library workspace are excluded from this estimate. The integer search and factorization are
+replicated on each MPI rank, not distributed sparse solvers.
+
+`SEED_NAME.little_group` appends the reference cell and operations, character/corepresentation rows,
+integer target, full atomic matrix, column site/local-representation provenance, and available
+signed/nonnegative witnesses. Local reference labels are separate from the earlier Gaussian-band
+labels; the recorded characters specify their meaning. An affirmative result is not proof of
+Bloch-bundle triviality; a negative result is not by itself a choice between a symmetry-enforced gap
+obstruction and an isolated topological band set.
+
+Gaussian regressions exercise GPW, GAPW, post-SCF SOC, a sheared cell, centering, screw phases,
+generic-point antiunitary pairing and a refined Wilson mesh. The one-band screw-monodromy example
+has no signed atomic signature; its two-band reference is atomically compatible. A zero-node TRIM
+case proves signed membership while leaving nonnegative membership unresolved. No existing physical
+reference values are changed. Native all-setting tests additionally change integer operation
+representatives and reorder operations and irrep columns, regenerating the factors independently;
+the band signature must remain invariant. Corrupted factors or missing operation matches are
+rejected before any integer decision.
+
+## Automatic reciprocal geometry
+
+The native `topology_reciprocal` module supplies the geometric sampling backend for
+`KPOINTS_SOURCE SYMMETRY`. In fractional coordinates an operation with direct rotation $W$ and
+antiunitary flag $a$ acts on reciprocal points through $(-1)^a W^{-T}$. The code enumerates the
+connected fixed tori and their nonempty intersections, using the existing saturated affine-torus
+solver. It identifies symmetry-equivalent closures, retains a generic point of each, and restores
+the full original stabilizer. Identical geometric actions are collapsed only during geometric
+enumeration. In particular, centering cosets, nonsymmorphic translations and an antiunitary PT
+stabilizer must remain in the little-group data used to construct representations.
+
+Every higher-symmetry specialization has an explicit group operation and integer reciprocal shift
+embedding its seed into the parent closure. A checked Smith factorization also supplies a primitive
+integer basis of each closure's tangent lattice. These are reciprocal torus cycles, not merely
+floating-point nullspace vectors. `reciprocal_sampling` returns representative points and directed
+incidence/cycle edges, retaining destination shifts for Bloch-phase/monodromy calculations. Child
+images are evaluated as explicit additional points, so this interface does not assume a convention
+for transporting local irrep labels between star arms.
+
+The native Gaussian analysis can be enabled in `DFT/PRINT/WANNIER90`, for example for an isolated
+two-band occupied prefix with a third computed band:
+
+```text
+KPOINTS_SOURCE SYMMETRY
+EXCLUDE_BANDS 3
+TIME_REVERSAL T
+LITTLE_GROUP_PATH_POINTS 3
+LITTLE_GROUP_MAX_POINTS 4096
+REQUIRE_GLOBAL_GAP T
+ATOMIC_SIGNATURES T
+```
+
+The band indices are illustrative: select the actual occupied space and compute excluded bands for
+the system of interest. SPGLIB and full three-dimensional periodicity are required. The calculation
+diagonalizes the frozen SCF Hamiltonian at each generated point; it does not change the SCF mesh or
+density, or reuse irreducible SCF orbitals. Both little-group and compatibility analysis are
+implicit. Atomic-signature comparison is optional. Scalar GPW/GAPW and the existing post-SCF GTH-SOC
+path share the same isolation, metric, subspace and sewing-law guards.
+
+`LITTLE_GROUP_PATH_POINTS` includes the endpoints and defaults to three. Uniform interior samples
+are wrapped and deduplicated, while each subedge retains the difference of its endpoint cell
+offsets. This preserves positive, negative and multiple boundary crossings and total winding.
+Increase this count to test sampling convergence. `LITTLE_GROUP_MAX_POINTS` is a conservative work
+budget before duplicate interiors are merged; it does not bound the geometric enumeration itself.
+`REQUIRE_GLOBAL_GAP` rejects a nonpositive **sampled** indirect gap, not an unobserved crossing
+between samples. A finite sample cannot prove global isolation or complete connectivity.
+
+The `.little_group` file begins with `AUTO_SIZES`, `AUTO_FAMILY`, `AUTO_CYCLE`, `AUTO_POINT` and
+`AUTO_EDGE` records. These retain closure normals/offsets, star sizes, primitive cycles, fractional
+coordinates and directed reciprocal shifts. Subsequent band and edge records use these point
+indices. Eigenvalues are written to `.eig`; `STATE_EXPORT` can additionally retain the AO states. No
+`.mmn` file is created: these ragged symmetry paths are not a Wannier90 neighbour mesh or a
+Wilson-loop surface. `WILSON_LOOP` with this source is rejected. Parity analysis may use included
+TRIM, but `INVERSION_TQC` still requires all relevant TRIM; star representatives alone need not
+contain them all. Use `KPOINTS_SOURCE TRIM` for a guaranteed complete inversion-only sample.
+
+The native tests check full spinful stabilizers, primitive cycles, lifted incidence segments,
+unimodular shears, arbitrary origin and coset shifts, operation permutations and rational reciprocal
+meshes. Induced scalar/spinful atomic columns must satisfy every generated unitary and antiunitary
+segment restriction. A database-free subset covers inversion/time reversal, PT, a magnetic half
+translation, a screw, centering and orthorhombic intersections. `--all-settings` extends this to all
+530 Hall settings, with and without time reversal.
+
+Gaussian regressions cover scalar He/GPW, all-electron He/GAPW, Ne/GTH-SOC and a consistently
+transformed sheared cell. Each case generates 15 fixed families, 56 points and 82 compatible
+segments. Local differential tests replay the same points and shifts through explicit NNKP input and
+compare eigenvalues, characters, segment restrictions and atomic witnesses. The path budget, invalid
+subdivision count, Wilson-mode conflict and a nonisolated single screw branch are tested as explicit
+rejections; refined sampling and the unitary-only mode are also checked.
+
+Connected fixed-torus closures are not necessarily connected open strata after higher-symmetry
+subsets are removed. The returned incidences and primitive cycles are not a complete equivariant
+cell complex, a global band-connectivity classification, or an EBR elementarity test.
+Conventional-cell folding is retained; primitive-cell equivalence remains a separate task.
+
+## Onsite reference correspondences
+
+`atomic_band_site_match` identifies a constructed site's local columns against a complete
+independent character table. The caller supplies Cartesian reference operations, their antiunitary
+flags, explicit spin lifts and an orthogonal map from reference axes to the native axes. Equal band
+dimensions or identical sampled induced signatures are not sufficient to assign onsite labels.
+
+The operation map is established geometrically. If the orientation has spin lift $V$, reference
+unitary matrices transform as $V U_g V^\dagger$, while the matrix part of an antiunitary operation
+transforms as $V A_g V^T$. Each $A_g$ includes the physical $i\sigma_y$ factor. The phase relating
+these matrices to the actual spin lifts retained by the native factor constructor fixes the
+character convention. It is not inferred from an arbitrary factor coboundary or fitted for each
+irrep. Scalar onsite representations use no spin phases.
+
+The matcher checks the full product/factor map, character completeness and a bijection of columns.
+For an antiunitary stabilizer, the supplied columns must be the unitary restrictions of complete
+corepresentations; paired and doubled restrictions are retained. Duplicate operations/columns,
+inconsistent lift/character gauges, missing spin time reversal and invalid axes are rejected.
+Failure leaves the output label map unallocated.
+
+The bounded native tests distinguish the one-dimensional C3 irreps, including paired/doubled grey
+extensions. A nonsymmorphic P6_3 example has a two-site orbit and C3 onsite symmetry: the same
+reference labels retain the analytic induced screw phases at three points along the reciprocal axis,
+also after a basis shear, Cartesian rotation, origin shift and operation reversal. Magnetic tests
+distinguish a local antiunitary C2\*T stabilizer from a time-reversed half translation that
+exchanges sites and imposes no antiunitary onsite restriction. The optional
+`topology_band_unittest --site-reference=FILE` sweep uses the Gamma subset of the independent
+character-reference fixture. It tests scalar/spinful point-group tables from all 230 groups in three
+coordinate conventions. Removing their translations realizes onsite point groups; this sweep is not
+a character-level check of every actual Wyckoff-site EBR.
+
+The additional `--wyckoff-reference=FILE` development sweep keeps the actual fractional space-group
+translations and supplies generic representatives of all 1,731 conventional Wyckoff families.
+Centering is removed by an explicitly recorded primitive basis. Scalar/spinful and ordinary/grey
+choices give 6,924 cases, each repeated after a simultaneous Cartesian rotation, lattice shear,
+origin shift, operation reversal and integer coset change. Complete onsite character tables come
+from the Gamma tables of 32 symmorphic point-group representatives. Their Cartesian orientation is
+recorded explicitly; it is not inferred from representation dimensions or induced band signatures.
+
+An independent Python orbit trace additionally checks the induced characters at six reciprocal
+points. It combines tabulated onsite characters, explicit orbit representatives, physical SU(2)
+intertwiners and Bloch translation phases. The comparison removes only the declared operation-lift
+and integer-coset convention changes. It checks all character entries and rejects incomplete output;
+the two-rank run repeats this audit on each process rather than distributing its algebra. The table
+precision tolerance is dimension-scaled for whole-band characters and does not relax the native
+construction tolerance. This covers real-site induction but not every position in a free Wyckoff
+family, all reciprocal strata, canonical Bilbao onsite axes/labels, or EBR elementarity. The large
+reference fixture is optional and is not installed or read in production calculations.
+
+This is a reference-matching kernel, not yet automatic standard labels in the Gaussian input path.
+It introduces no input keyword or runtime database dependency. A named reference correspondence does
+not by itself establish EBR elementarity, complete connectivity or material topology.
+
+### Site-resolved EBR character sequences
+
+The optional `topology_band_unittest --ebr-reference=FILE` compares induced columns against named
+EBR vectors combined with independent little-group character tables. A reference column must match
+the same native onsite column at every supplied point and operation. The test retains all matching
+columns rather than forcing a unique onsite label. Coincident signatures are compared as multisets,
+so duplicate references cannot consume the same single native column twice.
+
+The development fixture uses the ordinary-group scalar and spinful EBR data in `irreptables` 3.1.0
+for all 230 space groups. There are 1,599 site/spin cases, 5,641 reference columns, 9,667 reciprocal
+queries and 297,529 character entries per coordinate convention. Explicit type-I character tables
+supply 62 additional points in 40 tables, including the opposite valleys KA/HA and PA/WA. Their
+shared labelled characters agree with the original tables after checking the operation, spin-lift
+and integer-coset maps; no missing labels are inferred by complex conjugation. The adapter retains
+the source precision and the declared convention `k_native = -transpose(P) * k_table`.
+
+Both the original and combined sheared/rotated/origin-shifted/reordered conventions pass. Within the
+specified Wyckoff position, 5,263 columns have one candidate and 378 have multiple candidates at the
+tabulated points. This is sampled character matching, not a canonical onsite-axis identification, an
+isomorphism of Bloch bundles or a connectivity proof. The 5,704 native local columns at the selected
+sites also include 63 columns absent from the supplied reference; this test does not certify their
+elementarity. Grey-group EBR references and automatic Gaussian reference assignment are not part of
+this development sweep. No table is installed or read by a production calculation.
+
+The full invariant-torus Fourier comparison finds equal unitary character functions within all 378
+multi-candidate reference sets, in both conventions. Finer k sampling alone therefore cannot
+separate these cases. This does not assign canonical onsite labels or prove equivalent bundles. The
+Fourier sums are also evaluated against raw induction on every torsion component and three samples
+of the free coordinates. The `--all-settings` sweep covers all 530 Hall settings, scalar and spinful
+ordinary/grey groups at three sites, with 536,410 such queries including analytic controls. The
+separate Gaussian `ATOMIC_CHARACTER_CLASS` output is a diagnostic only.
 
